<div id="slideshow-holder"><div id="slideshow-wrapper"><div class="slide-container" id="slide1">
<div class="title-layout">
<div><span class="title-badge">Group 6 — Week 16</span></div>
<h1>Systems Acquisition</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Strategic Procurement, Process Lifecycle, Evaluation & Audit Control</p>
<div class="members-grid">
<div class="member-item">BARRA, ELISA MAE</div>
<div class="member-item">BORROMEO, CLARIZE</div>
<div class="member-item">IBARRETA, SHARMAINE</div>
<div class="member-item">INOCENTES, JUDY</div>
<div class="member-item">MALCO, ROMEO</div>
<div class="member-item">MAONG, FIONALYN</div>
<div class="member-item">PONTIPEDRA, CHRISTINA</div>
<div class="member-item">PRAXIDES, JHONEDEL</div>
<div class="member-item">RAMOS, MARY JOY</div>
<div class="member-item">SALUYA, HAZEL</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="speaker-notes-box">
<strong>Presenter Notes:</strong> Welcome standard audience. Today Group 6 presents Systems Acquisition—exploring how modern enterprises strategically select, purchase, evaluate, and audit critical information systems.
</div>
</div><div class="slide-container" id="slide2">
<h2 class="slide-title">Presentation Outline</h2>
<div class="content-area">
<div class="bullet-list" style="width: 100%; max-width: 900px;">
<ul>
<li>
<i class="fa-solid fa-compass"></i>
<strong>01. Importance of Acquisition Strategy:</strong> Understanding business alignment, total cost of ownership (TCO), and risk mitigation in software procurement.
</li>
<li>
<i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project"></i>
<strong>02. Systems Acquisition Process:</strong> A 4-stage breakdown from needs assessment to implementation and operational deployment.
</li>
<li>
<i class="fa-solid fa-chart-line"></i>
<strong>03. Planning & Evaluating Software:</strong> Technical feasibility, functional requirement matrices, and vendor selection criteria.
</li>
<li>
<i class="fa-solid fa-shield-halved"></i>
<strong>04. Auditor Involvement & Examinations:</strong> Governance, internal controls examination, and risk management during system acquisition.
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div class="speaker-notes-box">
<strong>Presenter Notes:</strong> This outline establishes our learning structure across four core modules required by our syllabus.
</div>
</div><div class="slide-container" id="slide3">
<h2 class="slide-title">Importance of Acquisition Strategy</h2>
<div class="content-area">
<div class="tiled-content">
<div class="tile">
<div class="icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-bullseye"></i></div>
<h3>Business Alignment</h3>
<p>Ensures technological investments directly support corporate objectives, operational workflows, and long-term organizational growth strategies.</p>
</div>
<div class="tile">
<div class="icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-scale-balanced"></i></div>
<h3>Risk & Compliance</h3>
<p>Identifies cyber vulnerabilities, data privacy mandates, and vendor lock-in risks before contract sign-off and capital commitment.</p>
</div>
<div class="tile">
<div class="icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-coins"></i></div>
<h3>TCO Optimization</h3>
<p>Prevents cost overruns by forecasting Total Cost of Ownership—including licensing, integration, user training, and recurring maintenance.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="speaker-notes-box">
<strong>Presenter Notes:</strong> Without a formal strategy, organizations suffer from redundant software purchases, integration bottlenecks, and unmanaged compliance exposure.
</div>
</div><div class="slide-container" id="slide4">
<h2 class="slide-title">Acquisition Strategy Pathways</h2>
<div class="content-area">
<div class="table-layout">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Strategy Path</th>
<th>Primary Advantage</th>
<th>Core Limitation</th>
<th>Ideal Context</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Custom Development (Build)</strong></td>
<td>Exact specification fit and full intellectual property control.</td>
<td>High upfront capital cost and lengthy development timeline.</td>
<td>Proprietary core competitive processes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Commercial Packaged (Buy)</strong></td>
<td>Lower cost, rapid deployment, proven industry standards.</td>
<td>Rigid customization limits; reliance on vendor roadmap.</td>
<td>Standard operations (e.g., HR, Accounting).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Outsourcing / Cloud (ASP/SaaS)</strong></td>
<td>Zero infrastructure setup and scalable pay-as-you-go pricing.</td>
<td>Potential security risk and loss of data governance control.</td>
<td>Rapid expansion or specialized niche utility.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="speaker-notes-box">
<strong>Presenter Notes:</strong> Choosing between building, buying off-the-shelf software, or outsourcing depends on organizational capability, urgency, and core competitive advantage.
</div>
</div><div class="slide-container" id="slide5">
<h2 class="slide-title">The System Acquisition Process</h2>
<div class="content-area">
<div class="timeline-layout">
<div class="timeline-item">
<div class="timeline-number">1</div>
<h3>Requirements Gathering</h3>
<p>Identify functional specifications, user workflows, and system constraints.</p>
</div>
<div class="timeline-item">
<div class="timeline-number">2</div>
<h3>Vendor Selection</h3>
<p>Issue RFP/RFI, evaluate solutions, and perform product demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<div class="timeline-item">
<div class="timeline-number">3</div>
<h3>Procurement</h3>
<p>Negotiate SLA terms, legal contracts, pricing, and licensing agreements.</p>
</div>
<div class="timeline-item">
<div class="timeline-number">4</div>
<h3>Deployment</h3>
<p>Configure software, conduct pilot tests, train users, and review post-launch.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="speaker-notes-box">
<strong>Presenter Notes:</strong> The acquisition lifecycle follows four sequential stages ensuring systematic risk control and clear milestone sign-offs.
</div>
</div><div class="slide-container" id="slide6">
<h2 class="slide-title">Planning Software Acquisitions</h2>
<div class="content-area">
<div class="two-column">
<div>
<div class="bullet-list">
<ul>
<li><i class="fa-solid fa-check-circle"></i> <strong>Feasibility Analysis:</strong> Evaluate financial, technical, legal, and operational viability prior to vendor engagement.</li>
<li><i class="fa-solid fa-check-circle"></i> <strong>Stakeholder Involvement:</strong> Form cross-functional teams including end-users, IT infrastructure experts, legal counsel, and finance.</li>
<li><i class="fa-solid fa-check-circle"></i> <strong>Architecture Integration:</strong> Ensure the software seamlessly interfaces with current databases, legacy systems, and API endpoints.</li>
<li><i class="fa-solid fa-check-circle"></i> <strong>Migration Strategy:</strong> Outline clear procedures for legacy data extraction, cleansing, and secure system transition.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div>
<div class="image-wrapper">
<img src="https://www.6sigma.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/stakeholder-analysis-matrix-png.webp" alt="Software evaluation matrix diagram">
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="speaker-notes-box">
<strong>Presenter Notes:</strong> Thorough planning eliminates scope creep and guarantees that software integrated matches enterprise infrastructure limits.
</div>
</div><div class="slide-container" id="slide7">
<h2 class="slide-title">Software Evaluation Weighting</h2>
<div class="content-area">
<div class="chart-container">
<div class="bar">
<div class="label">Functional Fit</div>
<div class="bar-inner"><div class="bar-fill" style="width: 35%; background: #4f46e5;">35% Weight</div></div>
</div>
<div class="bar">
<div class="label">Security & Compliance</div>
<div class="bar-inn